==Irish==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|sga|ga}} {{term|cen||besides; without|lang=sga}} < {{proto|Celtic|kina|besides|lang=ga}}; compare Welsh {{term|amgen|am-'''gen'''|otherwise|lang=cy}}, Breton {{term|ken||otherwise|lang=br}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|Munster}} {{IPA|lang=ga|[ɡɑn̪ˠ]}}
* {{a|Connacht|Ulster}} {{IPA|lang=ga|[ɡan̪ˠ]}}

===Preposition===
{{ga-prep}}

# [[without]]
# [[not]] {{qualifier|in conjunction with a verbal noun}}

====Usage notes====
Triggers lenition of ''b, c, g, m, p'' on unmodified nouns, e.g. '''gan {{l|ga|pingin|phingin}}''' "without a penny". Does not trigger lenition on modified nouns, e.g. '''gan {{l|ga|pingin}} {{l|ga|i|ina}} {{l|ga|póca|phóca}}''' "with a penny in his pocket". In the meaning "not", does not trigger lenition on either a verbal noun or on the direct object of the verbal noun, e.g. '''gan {{l|ga|ceannaigh|ceannach}}''' "not to buy", '''gan {{l|ga|pingin}} {{l|ga|a}} {{l|ga|saothraigh|shaothrú}}''' "not to earn a penny".

Unlike most Irish prepositions, '''gan''' is followed by the nominative case of nouns, not the dative, and it does not form prepositional pronouns: '''gan {{l|ga|an}} {{l|ga|arán|t-arán}}''' "without the bread", '''gan {{l|ga|mé}}''' "without me".

